WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.183
Picture Pedal Post, the bike courier in a small town.

2
00:00:06.183 --> 00:00:12.164
Its riders carry parcels across town on cargo bikes, and each rider has a handheld scanner.

3
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:23.445
Scan a parcel at a door, and the scanner sends a short message to Pedal Post's program: parcel PP-7781, collected, 2,500 grams.

4
00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:26.318
Three parts of the program want to hear it.

5
00:00:26.318 --> 00:00:28.167
The tracking page adds a line.

6
00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:30.818
The load board adds the weight to the bike.

7
00:00:30.818 --> 00:00:32.483
And the sender gets a text.

8
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.552
That message is an event: a note that something already happened.

9
00:00:36.552 --> 00:00:40.226
Over three sittings, we'll build a small event library for it.

10
00:00:40.226 --> 00:00:46.331
One part announces the news once, and the others react, without the announcer knowing who is listening.

11
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:51.361
And it's typed: the compiler knows exactly which kind of event each one is.

12
00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:54.312
We build it from the inside out.

13
00:00:54.312 --> 00:00:57.509
This one writes the model: a type for every event.

14
00:00:57.509 --> 00:01:02.623
The next one builds the bus, which carries each event to everyone who signed up.

15
00:01:02.623 --> 00:01:06.011
The last one gives it an API for Kotlin and for Java.

16
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:09.740
Now, a question that's easy to be shy about.

17
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.972
A scan is just three little fields.

18
00:01:11.972 --> 00:01:14.396
Each handler can read them for itself.

19
00:01:14.396 --> 00:01:17.968
So why build a whole model before anything even happens?

20
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:20.726
Let's try it without one.

21
00:01:20.726 --> 00:01:28.229
The demo has three handlers, small functions that each react to a scan, and each reads the raw fields for itself.

22
00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:29.689
Here's the load board.

23
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:35.736
If the type says collected, it turns the grams text into a number, right inside the handler.

24
00:01:35.736 --> 00:01:37.956
Java and Kotlin say the same thing.

25
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:41.315
Three scans go in.

26
00:01:41.315 --> 00:01:46.174
PP-7781 collected, then delivered, both 2,500 grams.

27
00:01:46.174 --> 00:01:55.611
Then PP-7782, collected, and the rider typed the weight as 2.5, because they weighed it in kilograms.

28
00:01:55.833 --> 00:01:57.598
Test scans are always tidy.

29
00:01:57.598 --> 00:02:02.238
Real scans are typed with gloves on, in the rain, at a stranger's door.

30
00:02:02.238 --> 00:02:05.178
And the scanner sends exactly what was typed.

31
00:02:05.178 --> 00:02:06.420
It has no opinions.

32
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:08.471
So, before we run it.

33
00:02:08.471 --> 00:02:11.140
Which scan fails, and in which handler?

34
00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.920
And how many texts go out?

35
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.297
The first two scans go fine: 2,500 grams onto the bike, then off again.

36
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.483
Then PP-7782.

37
00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:25.979
The tracking page lists it first.

38
00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:34.829
Then the load board tries to read 2.5 as a whole number, and throws a NumberFormatException, halfway through its job.

39
00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:43.083
So the tracking page has already told the world that PP-7782 is on a bike, and the load board never added it.

40
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.800
And texts sent: zero.

41
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:45.372
Not one.

42
00:02:45.372 --> 00:02:52.088
The text handler compares the type with deliver, one letter short, and nothing ever complains.

43
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:53.966
Two bugs from three scans.

44
00:02:53.966 --> 00:03:01.273
Each handler parses and checks for itself, so each one fails in its own way, and the compiler can't see any of it.

45
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.229
You might be thinking: easy, fix the typo and put a try around the number.

46
00:03:07.229 --> 00:03:09.540
Fair enough, that fixes these two.

47
00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:15.724
But where would a check have to go, so that PP-7782 never reaches the tracking page at all?

48
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.605
Not inside any one handler.

49
00:03:19.605 --> 00:03:21.373
The tracking page ran first.

50
00:03:21.373 --> 00:03:24.341
The check has to stand in front of all of them.

51
00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:27.458
Picture the depot without a clerk.

52
00:03:27.458 --> 00:03:31.904
A rider's note arrives in handwriting, and it goes from desk to desk.

53
00:03:31.904 --> 00:03:35.900
Each desk squints at the handwriting and reads it its own way.

54
00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.284
One desk can't read 2.5 and gives up.

55
00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:39.831
Another misreads a word.

56
00:03:39.831 --> 00:03:43.504
And the desk that opens next month brings its own squint.

57
00:03:44.433 --> 00:03:46.281
Now put one clerk at the door.

58
00:03:46.281 --> 00:03:53.303
The clerk copies each note onto a printed form, with a fixed set of boxes, and the units printed next to each box.

59
00:03:53.303 --> 00:03:59.216
If the note doesn't fit the form, the clerk sends it back, with a slip that says what was wrong.

60
00:04:00.233 --> 00:04:01.202
Let's map that.

61
00:04:01.202 --> 00:04:04.883
The handwritten note is the raw fields, a map of strings.

62
00:04:04.883 --> 00:04:06.368
Each desk is a handler.

63
00:04:06.368 --> 00:04:11.017
The printed form, with its fixed boxes, is a closed list of event types.

64
00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:13.988
The units are small types for ids and weights.

65
00:04:13.988 --> 00:04:16.700
And the clerk is one function at the door.

66
00:04:17.633 --> 00:04:19.725
Our picture breaks in one place.

67
00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:24.299
A real clerk could phone the rider: did you mean two and a half kilos?

68
00:04:24.299 --> 00:04:25.541
Ours never guesses.

69
00:04:25.541 --> 00:04:27.175
It refuses, and says why.

70
00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.268
Each of those three pieces gets its own section.

71
00:04:31.268 --> 00:04:35.889
A sealed interface: the closed list, so the compiler knows every case.

72
00:04:35.889 --> 00:04:40.509
Value classes: a ParcelId and a Weight that can't even be built wrong.

73
00:04:40.509 --> 00:04:46.516
And parseEvent at the door, which hands back Ok with an event, or Rejected with the reason.

74
00:04:47.433 --> 00:04:50.655
Behind that door, no handler reads a raw field again.

75
00:04:50.655 --> 00:04:53.025
Nobody squints at handwriting any more.

76
00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:56.733
The load board gets a Weight in whole grams, already checked.

77
00:04:57.633 --> 00:05:03.249
So look in your own code for a map of raw string fields handed to more than one function.

78
00:05:03.249 --> 00:05:06.971
Each of those functions is a desk squinting at handwriting.

79
00:05:06.971 --> 00:05:09.621
Keep the checks in one place: at the door.

80
00:05:10.533 --> 00:05:13.394
So, why build a model before anything happens?

81
00:05:13.394 --> 00:05:19.178
Because a raw scan gets read once per handler, and every reading is a new chance to be wrong.

82
00:05:19.178 --> 00:05:22.225
Parse once, at the door, into an event or a note.

83
00:05:22.225 --> 00:05:24.277
Behind the door, trust the types.

84
00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:25.310
Quick check.

85
00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:31.654
Next month a fourth handler arrives, a weekly report, and it reads the grams field for itself.

86
00:05:31.654 --> 00:05:33.408
Can the 2.5 scan break it?

87
00:05:33.408 --> 00:05:36.985
And with the door in place, what does the report get?

88
00:05:40.167 --> 00:05:44.902
Without the door, yes: it squints at the same handwriting, in its own way.

89
00:05:44.902 --> 00:05:47.014
With the door, it never sees 2.5.

90
00:05:47.014 --> 00:05:51.045
It only gets events whose Weight was checked once, at the door.
